{# A signpost, not a form. Nothing here is submitted to this site: every
   link goes to the authority that owns the problem, because a report
   logged in their system gets a reference number and a tracked repair,
   where a report to a website gets neither.

   Categories are added only once their route has been verified live.
   Two at launch. The temptation is to list everything and check later;
   a dead link on this page is worse than a missing category, because a
   reader who follows one and gets nowhere does not come back. #}

<div class="report-emergency">
  <p><strong>If someone is in danger, phone 999.</strong></p>
  <p>For a road or pavement fault that is an immediate danger — deep hole,
  collapsed surface, flooding across a carriageway — North Ayrshire ask you
  to phone them rather than use a form: <strong>01294 310000</strong>.
  Traffic light faults go to the same number.</p>
</div>

<h2>Roads and pavements</h2>
<div class="report-item">
  <p>Potholes, broken kerbs, damaged pavements, flooding on a public road.</p>
  <p><a href="https://www.north-ayrshire.gov.uk/roads-and-parking/report-road-fault">Report a road fault to North Ayrshire Council →</a></p>
  <p class="report-tip"><strong>Not the A78.</strong> The main coast road
  through Largs is a trunk road, maintained by Amey for Transport Scotland,
  not by North Ayrshire. A report about the A78 sent to the council will be
  passed on rather than acted on, so it is quicker to go straight to
  <a href="https://www.swtrunkroads.scot/">South West Trunk Roads</a>.</p>
  <p class="report-tip">The council aim to inspect within five working days.
  Photographs help — one close up, one wide enough to show where it is.</p>
</div>

<h2>Street lights</h2>
<div class="report-item">
  <p>A light out, flickering, or on during the day.</p>
  <p><a href="https://www.north-ayrshire.gov.uk/roads-and-parking/report-street-light-fault">Report a street light fault to North Ayrshire Council →</a></p>
  <p class="report-tip"><strong>Find the number on the column.</strong> Every
  street light carries its own number. Quoting it means the fault can be
  passed to the contractor without anyone having to go and find the light
  first.</p>
  <p class="report-tip">Exposed wires, a missing door on the column, accident
  damage, or four or more lights out in a row count as an emergency — phone
  01294 310000 instead.</p>
</div>

<h2 class="report-lcc-head">When to bring it to the Community Council</h2>
<div class="report-lcc">
  <p>Largs Community Council cannot repair a road or a street light — no
  community council can. What it can do is press the people who can.</p>
  <p>That works best when a fault has already been reported and has a
  reference number. Every report above produces one by email. A reference
  number turns "a resident mentioned a pothole" into a specific case with a
  date on it, which is a far stronger thing for the Community Council to
  raise on your behalf.</p>
  <p>So: <strong>report it first, keep the reference, and bring that along
  if nothing happens.</strong> The Community Council also takes up matters
  that are not faults at all — a junction that keeps having near misses, a
  path everyone uses that nobody maintains, a service the town is losing.
  Those belong at a meeting from the start.</p>
  <p><a href="/council/">More about the Community Council and what it has been dealing with →</a></p>
</div>

<p class="report-note">This page is a signpost. Nothing typed into the linked
forms comes to this site, and this site cannot see what happens to a report
once it is made. Links checked on 15 August 2026 — if one has stopped
working, please <a href="/colophon/">let me know</a>.</p>
